{
  "concept": "Carbon-13 chemical shifts fall into broad, overlapping ranges characteristic of each carbon environment, so a peak's position on the reversed δ scale narrows an assignment to a family of possibilities rather than naming one group outright.",
  "figureType": "diagram",
  "elements": [
    "Pale yellow vertical gradient background panel (warmer at top, near-white at bottom) with thin dark border",
    "Horizontal axis beneath the panel labeled 'Chemical shift (δ)', reversed: 220 at far left decreasing to 0 at far right",
    "Major ticks with numeric labels every 20 ppm: 220, 200, 180, 160, 140, 120, 100, 80, 60, 40, 20, 0",
    "Unit label 'ppm' printed to the right of the 0 tick",
    "Faint vertical gridlines rising from each labeled tick through the panel",
    "Vertical axis label 'Intensity' in rotated text at left, with a magenta/pink arrow pointing upward beside it (no numeric scale)",
    "Gray-beige shaded bar 'C=O' spanning ~220–160 ppm (bottom row)",
    "Gray-beige bar 'C=C' spanning ~150–107 ppm",
    "Gray-beige bar 'Aromatic' spanning ~148–110 ppm, one row above C=C",
    "Gray-beige bar 'C≡N' spanning ~130–120 ppm",
    "Gray-beige bar 'C≡C' spanning ~95–80 ppm",
    "Gray-beige bar 'C—O' spanning ~88–68 ppm",
    "Gray-beige bar 'C—N' spanning ~78–55 ppm",
    "Long gray-beige bar 'C—Hal' spanning ~80–10 ppm",
    "Gray-beige bar '—CH—' spanning ~60–42 ppm",
    "Gray-beige bar '—CH2—' spanning ~50–28 ppm",
    "Gray-beige bar '—CH3' spanning ~30–18 ppm (top row)",
    "Centered serif label text inside each bar (short bars may carry the label with small flanking bond dashes)",
    "Interactive vertical cursor line with a draggable handle and a δ readout bubble",
    "Side panel list titled 'Carbons that absorb here' that fills with the names of bars intersected by the cursor",
    "Hover tooltip showing a bar's ppm endpoints, plus a translucent highlight band projected down onto the axis for that bar's range",
    "Annotation arrow/caption reminding the reader that 'deshielded = larger δ = farther LEFT'"
  ],
  "interactions": [
    {
      "id": "shiftCursor",
      "type": "slider",
      "label": "Observed peak position (δ, ppm)",
      "range": [0, 220, 1],
      "default": 120,
      "teaches": "A single observed 13C peak can be consistent with several carbon environments at once, because the characteristic ranges overlap; the cursor shows exactly which families are still in play."
    },
    {
      "id": "bandInspect",
      "type": "hover",
      "teaches": "Each functional group occupies a definite ppm window, and groups whose carbon is bonded to O, N, or halogen sit at higher δ (farther left) because those electronegative atoms deshield the carbon."
    }
  ],
  "demo_steps": [
    {
      "title": "Read the reversed scale",
      "narration": "The axis runs backwards: 220 ppm on the left, 0 ppm on the right. Every gray bar marks the window where one kind of carbon absorbs; bar height is only stacking, not intensity.",
      "state": { "shiftCursor": 220 }
    },
    {
      "title": "Alkane country (10–40 ppm)",
      "narration": "Park the cursor near 25 ppm and only the plain sp3 bars — CH3, CH2, and C—Hal — light up. Unsubstituted carbons hug the right end of the chart.",
      "state": { "shiftCursor": 25 }
    },
    {
      "title": "A crowded, ambiguous region (70 ppm)",
      "narration": "At 70 ppm the cursor cuts C—O, C—N, C—Hal, C≡C, and —CH— all at once. One peak here does not identify a group; it narrows the field and you need other evidence to choose.",
      "state": { "shiftCursor": 70 }
    },
    {
      "title": "The sp2 / aromatic band (125 ppm)",
      "narration": "Slide left to 125 ppm and the alkane bars go dark while C=C, Aromatic, and C≡N light up. Doubly bonded and ring carbons cluster in a band of their own.",
      "state": { "shiftCursor": 125 }
    },
    {
      "title": "Carbonyl carbons stand alone",
      "narration": "Past 160 ppm only C=O remains. A peak above 160 ppm is nearly diagnostic — the most deshielded carbon in an ordinary organic molecule is the one double-bonded to oxygen.",
      "state": { "shiftCursor": 190 }
    },
    {
      "title": "Inspect one band",
      "narration": "Hover any bar to read its endpoints and see its window projected onto the axis. Compare C—Hal's huge span against CH3's narrow one: some environments are far more diagnostic than others.",
      "state": { "shiftCursor": 120 }
    }
  ],
  "keyInsight": "A 13C chemical shift is an overlapping range, not an address: the farther left (higher δ) a carbon appears, the more it has been deshielded by π bonding or an electronegative neighbor, and assignment means intersecting that position with every band it touches.",
  "reconstructionNotes": "Wide landscape figure. Pale yellow-to-white vertical gradient fills the plotting panel, thin dark rule around it. Axis under the panel is reversed (220 left → 0 right) with labeled ticks every 20 ppm, faint light-gray vertical gridlines through the panel, axis title 'Chemical shift (δ)' centered below and 'ppm' just right of the 0 tick. Left side: rotated word 'Intensity' with a bold magenta upward arrow, no numbers — make clear that vertical position is only stacking. Eleven warm gray-beige rounded rectangles of uniform height, each horizontally positioned by its ppm span and stacked lowest-to-highest as: C=O (220–160), C=C (150–107), Aromatic (148–110), C≡N (130–120), C≡C (95–80), C—O (88–68), C—N (78–55), C—Hal (80–10), —CH— (60–42), —CH2— (50–28), —CH3 (30–18). Serif dark labels centered inside bars; short bars use small flanking dashes as in the original. Interactive layer: a vertical cursor line (magenta, matching the arrow) with a drag handle and a δ value bubble at the top; bars it crosses switch to a saturated highlight fill with darker outline while non-intersected bars fade to low opacity. A right-hand or lower-right list panel names the currently intersected groups. Hovering a bar shows a small tooltip '92–80 ppm' style readout and paints a translucent band of the same color down over the axis strip between the two endpoints, with dotted drop lines to the tick scale. Include a small caption near the left of the axis reading 'deshielded ← higher δ' to reinforce the reversed direction."
}

